# End-to-end tests

These run {{ .ProviderName }} against a kind control plane with Crossplane
installed and the provider deployed from a locally built image.

```
test/
├── setup.sh            # runs once before the tests; applies the ProviderConfig + Secret
└── behavior/           # controller behaviour, plain chainsaw tests (created by create-test)
```

The managed resource lifecycle — create, observe, delete — is driven by
`examples/<group>/<kind>.yaml`, one file per kind. [uptest](https://github.com/crossplane/uptest)
reads the `uptest.upbound.io/*` annotations on each one; the Makefile picks
them up by wildcard, so nothing needs wiring once the file exists.

## Producing an example for a kind

`create api` cannot seed one: nothing about a valid `spec.forProvider` is
knowable before the Terraform schema is generated. After `make generate`:

1. Copy `examples-generated/namespaced/<group>/<version>/<kind>.yaml` to
   `examples/<group>/<kind>.yaml`.
2. Resolve any `${...}` Terraform interpolations (e.g. `file()`,
   `filebase64()`) — the scraped doc example does not resolve them for you.
3. Keep `namespace: crossplane-system` and `providerConfigRef: {name: default,
   kind: ProviderConfig}` — that's where the scaffolded credentials Secret and
   ProviderConfig live. Worked example:
   [docs/upjet-provider.md §6](https://github.com/cychiang/xp-provider-gen/blob/main/docs/upjet-provider.md#6-worked-example-managing-a-configmap-with-hashicorpkubernetes).
4. Add `uptest.upbound.io/timeout` and `uptest.upbound.io/conditions:
   "Ready,Synced"` annotations under `metadata`.

## Running

```bash
make e2e              # full flow: build image, kind + Crossplane, deploy, uptest
make test-behavior    # chainsaw behavior tests only, against a live cluster
make e2e-clean        # delete the e2e kind cluster when you're done
```

## Adding a behavior test

Once a kind has an example:

```bash
xp-provider-gen create-test --name drift-check --kind MyKind
```

These files are yours: the generator seeds them once and never overwrites them.
